\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces The S2ELab preparation pipeline. Extraction keeps the pattern $p$ with its match list $[w_1]$ and non-match list $[w_2]$; $\mathit  {dedup}$ drops byte-identical patterns and inputs; $\mathit  {strip}(p,[\texttt  {\textasciicircum },\texttt  {\textdollar }])$ yields the translated \texttt  {Regex} $r$. The check calls $\mathit  {parse}(r,w)$ on each input and keeps the pair only when the verdict agrees with its label (\text  {$\mathsurround \z@ \mathchar "458$}), validating the pattern's own semantics rather than real-world correctness: \texttt  {"55/5/3434"} is accepted because the pattern checks digit counts, not value ranges. Surviving triples are exactly the defined entries $(r,w,e)$ with $e \in \{\mathit  {true}, \mathit  {false}\}$.}}{69}{figure.caption.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:s2elab-pipeline}{{8.1}{69}{The S2ELab preparation pipeline. Extraction keeps the pattern $p$ with its match list $[w_1]$ and non-match list $[w_2]$; $\mathit {dedup}$ drops byte-identical patterns and inputs; $\mathit {strip}(p,[\texttt {\textasciicircum },\texttt {\textdollar }])$ yields the translated \texttt {Regex} $r$. The check calls $\mathit {parse}(r,w)$ on each input and keeps the pair only when the verdict agrees with its label (\checkmark ), validating the pattern's own semantics rather than real-world correctness: \texttt {"55/5/3434"} is accepted because the pattern checks digit counts, not value ranges. Surviving triples are exactly the defined entries $(r,w,e)$ with $e \in \{\mathit {true}, \mathit {false}\}$}{figure.caption.48}{}}
